    "ls|LS17_S3_U_127": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION N\nO. 127\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY 3\nRD\n \nJANUARY, 2020 MAGHA 14\n, 1941 (SAKA)\n \nINCOME TAX EXEMPTION\n \n127.\n \nJASBIR SINGH GILL\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nFINANCE \nbe pleased to \nstate:\n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether it is a fact that the Government has decided any limit for exemption of Income Tax;\n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(c)\n \nthe details of limit of exemption of Income Tax before the general election of 2019; and\n \n(d)\n \nthe\n \ntime by which exemption limit of Rs.5 lacs would be implemented?\n \n                                                      \n \n                                                   \n  \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF FINANCE\n \n \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR)\n \n \n(a) and (b):\n \nThe\n \nFinance Act of respective years, \ninter\n-\nalia\n, provides for the rates of income\n-\ntax as well as \nthe \nbasic exemption limit on the income of individuals, \nHindu undivided family\n, \nassociation of persons or \nbody of individuals,\n \nartificial juridical person \nas defined under the Income\n-\ntax Act, 1961 (the Act).  The \nFinance Bill\n \nprior to its enactment as an Act,\n \nis tabled before the Parliament \nduring the Union Budget which \nis being presented every year in Lok Sabha on 1\nst\n \nFebruary si\nnce 2017\n.\n \n \n(c)\n \nBefore the general election of 2019, the exemption limits were provided under the Finance Act, 2019 \nand were the same as the basic exemption limits as provided under the Finance (No. 2) Act, 2019 which is \nas below:\n \n \nNature of Person\n \nBasic \nExemption\n \nIncome Level\n \nIndividual (other than senior and very senior \ncitizen), HUF, association of persons, body \nof individuals and artificial juridical person.\n \nRs 2,50,000 /\n-\n \nIndividual, resident in India who is of the age \nof sixty years or more but \nless than eighty \nyears.\n \n(senior citizen)\n \nRs 3,00,000\n \nIndividual, resident in India who is of the age \nof eighty years or more\n.\n \n(very senior citizen)\n \nRs 5,00,000\n \n \n(d)\n \nVide Finance Act, 2019, section 87A of the Act was amended to provide a 100% tax rebate to \nindividuals having total income up to Rs. 5 lakhs. \n \n                                              \n------\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_128": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 128 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.02.2020 \nLAVATORIES IN HOUSES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n*128.  SHRI ARVIND DHARMAPURI: \n \nWill the Minister of  RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the houses constructed under Pradhan M antri Awas Yojana (PMAY-G) do \nnot have any lavatories; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the numb er of such houses identified; \n(c)  the reasons therefor; \n(d)  the amount actually sanctioned for PMAY-G hous es during the last five years to the \nState of Telangana; and \n(e)  the steps being taken by the Government for pr oviding lavatories in the said houses? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *128 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11.02.2020 REGA RDING \nLAVATORIES IN HOUSES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n(a) to (e): As per the Framework for Implementation  (FFI) of Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-\nGramin (PMAY-G), construction of a toilet has been made an integral part of the PMAY-G \nhouse. The PMAY-G house is treated as complete only  after the toilet has been constructed. \nProvision has been made for an assistance of Rs. 12 ,000 for construction of toilets to eligible \nbeneficiaries through funding from Swachh Bharat Mi ssion-Gramin (SBM-G), Mahatama \nGandhi National Rural Employment Scheme (MGNREGS) o r any other dedicated financing \nsource. \n \nAn amount of Rs.247.88 crore was released to the St ate of Telangana during 2015-16 under \nthe erstwhile rural housing scheme Indira Awaas Yoj ana (IAY). Thereafter, PMAY-G was \nlaunched w.e.f. 01.04.2016. An amount of Rs. 190.79  Crore was released to the State of \nTelangana as 1 \nst  instalment of 2016-17 under PMAY-G. No funds have been released to the \nState thereafter. Neither the physical progress has  been shown by the State on AwaasSoft, \nMIS of PMAY-G, the only source of reporting progres s, nor utilisation certificates have been \nreceived from the State so far. Accordingly, State has been requested to surrender the fund \nreleased under PMAY-G.  \n \n**************** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_129": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *129 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11TH FEBRUARY, 2020  \n \nUpgradation of Pisciculture Infrastructure \n \n \n*129. SHRI PINAKI MISHRA:  \n       \n   \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING मत्स्यपाऱन, \nपशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether any plans are in place to upgrade the physical infrastructure for pisciculture in the \ncountry, especially with respect to cage culture; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government is proposing to extend marketing support to States for the export of \nfish to boost the revenue of the fisheries sector; and \n \n (d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n (SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n(a)  to  (d)  :      A Statement is placed on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nStatement referred to in reply to the Lok Sabha Starred Question No.*129 put in by Shri \nPinaki Mishra , Member of Parliament , Lok Sabha for answer on 11 th February, 2020 \nregarding Upgradation of Pisciculture Infrastructure \n \n(a) and (b) : Yes, Sir. The Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying plans to upgrade the \nphysical infrastructure for pisciculture in the country and is implementing various \nschemes/programmes for development of fisheries including creation of infrastructure . Details of the \nCentral Schemes being implemented are as below:  \n \n(i) The Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) on Blue Revolution: Integrated Development and \nManagement of Fisheries at a t otal central outlay of Rs.  3000 crore  is being  implemented \nsince 2015 -16 for development of fisheries in the country. The CSS inter-alia, provides \nfinancial assistance for creation of infrastructure for pisciculture such as installation of cages \nin reservoirs and open sea, cages for fish culture in brackishwater, establishment of fish brood \nbanks, hatcheries, construction of ponds, rearing ponds, fish feed mills, development of post-\nharvest infrastructure like ice plants,  cold storages, ice plants-cum-cold storages and \ndevelopment of fish landing centre  in reservoirs.  Details of the physical infrastructure \nfacilities taken up under the CSS for pisciculture  activities in the country, especially with \nrespect to cage culture are furnished at Annexure-I. \n \n(ii) A new scheme titled Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Fund (FIDF) was launched in \n2019-20 with a fund size of Rs . 7522.48 crore for providing access to concessional finance \nfor development fisheries infrastructure in the country.  Under the FIDF, the Mini stry of \nFisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying provides interest subvention up to 3% per annum \nfor providing the concessional finance by the Nodal Loaning Entities for development of \ninfrastrucutre in the fisheries sector.   \n \n(c) and (d) :  Yes, Sir. Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying, Government of India is \nextending monitory support to the States and Union Territories under the Blue Revolution: Integrated \nDevelopment and Management of Fisheries  Scheme. The scheme provides financial assistance to the \nState Governments and Union Territories  for creation of  marketing infrastructure facilities for \nfisheries such as retail fish markets, mobile fish hubs, retail fish outlets (Kiosks), fish preservation and \nfish transport vehicles.  The Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Development Fund (FIDF) also \nprovides for development of modern fish markets, fish processing units, fish preservation, fish \ntransport and cold chain network infrastructure . Besides, this Marine Products Export Development  \nAuthority ( MPEDA), Ministry of Commerce and Industry  is implementing various schemes and \ntraining programmes for increasing the unit value of the production and export of value -added marine \nproducts from the country and strengthening the capture fisheries sector to enhance the compliance of \nimporting country requirements. \n \n***** \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure- I \n \nStatement referred to in reply to part (a) and (b) of the Lok Sabha Question No. 129 for answer on \n11th February, 2020 regarding Upgradation of Pisciculture Infrast ructure: Details of Pisciculture \nrelated projects/activities taken up under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) on Blue \nRevolution: Integrated Development and Management of Fisherie. \n \n \na) 770 nos of sea cage for fish culture  \nb) 25,625 hactres area brought under aquaculture \nc) 522 nos of Re-circulatory Aquaculture Systems (RAS) \nd) 13,982 nos of cages in reservoirs and other  water bodies \ne) 535 nos of fish/prawn hatcheries \nf) 229 nos fish feed mill/plants \ng) 2026 nos. Raceways for Coldwater Fisheries \nh) Construction of 140 nos of landing centers including Reservoirs and rivers \ni) 11 nos Brood-bank facilities created \nj) 461 units of post -Harvest Infrastructure facilities viz., ice plants and cold storage have been \nsanctioned \nk) 10,111 units of fish transportation facilities viz., refrigerat ed and insulated trucks, auto \nrickshaws, motor cycles and bicycles with ice box \nl) 1,218 units of fish markets and fish mobile markets \nm) Skill development/training of 85,274 fishers, fish farmers and other stakeholders \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_13": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 13  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nLAUNCH OF PORTAL SANTUSHT \n  \n13. SHRI GAURAV GOGOI:  \n      SHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR: \n  \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT  be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)whether the Government proposes/has launched a portal ‘Santusht’ for \nspeedy redressal of workers as well as employer grievances and \nensuring implementation of labour laws at the grassroot level including \nmonitoring of ESIC, EPFO, etc.;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof and implementation status thereof along with \nresponse received so far; \n(c)whether the Government has any alternate means to resolve such \ngrievances amongst workers and employers who do not have access \nto computer or internet; and \n(d)if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (d): ‘Santusht’ - Implementation Monitoring Cell (IMC) has been \nconstituted in the Office of Minister of State (Independent Charge) for \nLabour & Employment in January 2020. The objective of ‘Santusht’ is to \npromote transparency, accountability, effective delivery of public \nservices and implementation of policies, schemes of Ministry of Labour \nand Employment at grassroot level through constant monitoring. \nFor public grievances, Centralized Public Grievance Redress \nand Monitoring System  (CPGRAM) portal is already functional. Besides, \nwritten grievances as received from stakeholders are also disposed by \nthe Ministry. F urther, online portal under ‘Santusht’ for public grievances \nhas not been started so far. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_130": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 30 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nNGOs Working for MDMS \n \n \n30. SHRI SELVAM G: \n    SHRI GAUTAM SIGAMAANI PON: \n    DR. HEENA GAVIT: \n    SHRI REBATI TRIPURA: \n    SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the NGOs are providing meals to schools through centralised kitchens under \nMid-Day Meal (MDM) scheme and if so, the number thereof, State/ UT-wise;  \n \n(b) the number of Rural and Urban schools covered by NGOs, State/UT -wise especially \nKanchipuram district of Tamil Nadu;  \n \n(c) whether the samples collected from NGO’s centralised kitchen have been found to be \nin compliance with prescribed standards and nutriti ve values and if so, the details \nthereof and if not, the reasons therefor and the steps taken in this direction;  \n \n      (d) whether it is a fact that NGO’s engaged in preparing mid -day meals for children in \nrural areas have turned the scheme into a profit  making exercise and deviated from the \npurpose and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(e) whether the Government is considering to eliminate the role of NGOs in preparing \nmid-day meal in rural areas and if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the State \nGovernments thereto; and  \n \n(f) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government to ensure mid -day meal are \nmandatorily tested by accredited labs each month for maintaining quality and nutrition? \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’)   \n \n(a)  & (b): The Mid -Day Meal Scheme (MDMS) is implemented in partnership with \nStates and UTs. The overall responsibility for providing cooked and nutritious mid -\nday meal to the eligible children lies with the State Governments and Union \nTerritory Administrations. State and UT Governments engage NGOs / Voluntary \nOrganizations for preparing meals in Centralized kitchens for identified clusters of \nschools. As per the information provided by the States and UTs, at present 17 States \nand UTs have engaged NGOs / Voluntary Organizations under the Scheme.  In the \nState of Tamil Nadu no NGOs have been engaged for providing meals in schools.  \nThe State and UT-wise details of NGOs engaged and the numbers of rural and urban \nschools covered by NGOs under the Scheme are given at Annexure-I. \n   \n(c) to (f):  The Government of India has issued guidelines on quality, safety and hygiene \nin school level kitchens to all the States and UTs to address the quality issue. These \nguidelines inter -alia provide that the States/UTs must consider engaging CSIR \nInstitutes / N ABL accredited laboratories and FSSAI accredited laboratories for \nundertaking testing of samples of MDM for food safety and contamination. Further, \nthe MDM Rules, 2015 provide for mandatory testing of food samples by \nGovernment recognized laboratories to e nsure that the meals meet nutritional \nstandards and quality. As per the information provided by the States and UTs, 1483 \nsamples have been collected from schools covered by NGOs  and all are found in \ncompliance with the norms prescribed under the Scheme. T he State and UT -wise \ndetails of these samples are given at Annexure-II.  \nStates and UTs have not reported any case of engagement of NGOs those have \nturned the scheme into a profit making exercise in rural areas across the country.  As \nper revised guidelines  for engagement of Civil Society Organizations  (CSO) /Non \nGovt. Organizations (NGO) in Mid-Day-Meal Scheme.  CSO/NGO may be engaged \nfor supply of hot cooked meal under MDMS for a cluster of schools in urban areas \nor in identified rural areas which have goo d road connectivity, for the purpose of \nleveraging efficiency gains. \n******** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 30 for \n03.02.2020 raised by Shri Selvam G, Shri Gautam Sigamaani Pon, Dr. Heena Gavit, Shri \nRebati Tripura, Shri Dhanush M. Kumar regarding NGOs Working for MDMS \n \nDetails of NGOs engaged and the numbers of rural and urban schools covered by NGOs under \nMid-Day Meal Scheme \n \nS. \nNo. State / UT \nNumber of \nNGOs \nproviding \nMDM \nNumber of \ndistricts in \nwhich NGOs \nare serving \nMDM \nNumber of schools \ncovered by NGOs \nRural Urban Total \n1 Andhra Pradesh 7 13 3575 1358 4933 \n2 Assam 1 2 402 143 545 \n3 Bihar 6 12 856 1842 2698 \n4 Chhattisgarh 5 5 0 681 681 \n5 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 1 1 265 15 280 \n6 Daman & Diu 1 1 47 15 62 \n7 Delhi 52 13 0 2951 2951 \n8 Gujarat 3 9 1959 1443 3402 \n9 Haryana 1 4 1520 407 1927 \n10 Jharkhand 1 2 176 200 376 \n11 Karnataka 66 13 2361 2458 4819 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 23 19 0 3740 3740 \n13 Maharashtra 4 10 789 2605 3394 \n14 Odisha 6 12 2466 952 3418 \n15 Rajasthan 3 9 2291 1705 3996 \n16 Telangana 2 7 1160 938 2098 \n17 Uttar Pradesh 112 26 4634 2888 7522 \n  Total 294 158 22501 24341 46842 \n \n  \n\n \nAnnexure-II \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (c ) to (f) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 30 \nfor 03.02.2020 raised by Shri Selvam G, Shri Gautam Sigamaani Pon, Dr. Heena Gavit, \nShri Rebati Tripura, Shri Dhanush M. Kumar regarding NGOs Working for MDMS \n \nDetails of samples collected from schools covered by NGOs  under Mid-Day Meal \nScheme \n \nS. \nNo. State / UT \nNumber of \nNGOs \nproviding \nMDM \nNumber of \nsamples \ncollected from \nschools \ncovered by \nNGOs \nNumber of \nsamples \nfound in \ncompliance \nwith norms \n1 Andhra Pradesh 7 0 0 \n2 Assam 1 12 12 \n3 Bihar 6 29 29 \n4 Chhattisgarh 5 10 10 \n5 Gujarat 3 78 78 \n6 Haryana 1 68 68 \n7 Jharkhand 1 0 0 \n8 Karnataka 66 95 95 \n9 Madhya Pradesh 23 12 12 \n10 Maharashtra 4 42 42 \n11 Odisha 6 2 2 \n12 Rajasthan 3 12 12 \n13 Telangana 2 41 41 \n14 Uttar Pradesh 112 156 156 \n15 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 1 0 0 \n16 Daman & Diu 1 0 0 \n17 Delhi 52 926 926 \n  Total 294 1483 1483 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_131": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.131 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nTOURISM CIRCUITS UNDER SWADESH DARSHAN SCHEME \n \n131. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY:  \nSHRI SANJAY SETH:  \nSHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the names of the tourism circuits in Jharkhand and Andhra \nPradesh which have been selected under the Swadesh \nDarshan scheme;  \n(b)  the names of the places which have been selected under \nthese circuits;  \n(c)  the details of amount sanctioned for carrying out works under \nthe scheme; and  \n(d)  the time by which these works are likely to be completed? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) to (d): The details of the projec t sanctioned in Andhra Pradesh \nand state of Jharkhand under the Swadesh Darshan scheme is \nplaced below:- \n \nState \n/Year of \nsanction \nDetail Sanctioned \n(Crores) \nProject \nstatus \nTimeline \nof the \nproject \nAndhra \nPradesh \n2014-15 \n \nDevelopment of  \nKakinada - Hope \nIsland - Coringa \nWildlife Sanctuary - \nPassarlapudi - Aduru \n67.84 100% Completed \n\n- S Yanam - Kotipally \nunder Coastal Circuit \nAndhra \nPradesh \n2015-16 \n \nDevelopment of \nNellore - Pulikat Lake \n- Ubblamadugu Water \nFalls – Nelapattu- \nKothakoduru- Mypadu \n- Ramateertham - \nIskapalli under \nCoastal Circuit \n59.70 90% January \n2020 \nAndhra \nPradesh  \n2017-18 \n \nDevelopment of: \nShalihundam-\nThotlakonda- \nBavikonda- \nBojjanakonda- \nAmravati- Anupu \nunder Buddhist \nCircuit \n52.34 45% March \n2020 \nJharkhand \n2018-19 \n \nDevelopment of \nDalma- Chandil- \nGetalsud- Betla \nNational park - \nMirchaiya- Netarhat \nunder Eco Circuit \n52.72 0% 18 Months \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_132": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *132 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 11TH FEBRUARY, 2020/ MAGHA 22, 1941 (SAKA) \n \nTUKDE TUKDE GANG \n \n*132.   SHRI JASBIR SINGH GILL: \n SHRI VINCENT H. PALA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether an organisation called the ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’ has been identified \nand catalogued by the Ministry of Home Affairs, National Crime Records Bureau \n(NCRB) or any Central/State Law Enforcement Agency/Police Force or any \nCentral or State Intelligence Structures; \n \n(b) whether the terminology ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’ is based on specific inputs \nprovided by the Ministry or other law enforcement/intelligence agencies; \n \n(c) whether the Ministry of Home Affairs/NCRB or any other law enforcement or \nIntelligence organisation has drawn up a list of alleged leaders and members of \nthe ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’; \n \n(d) whether any penal action/punishment (and specifically under which sections \nof the IPC and/or other Acts) by the Ministry of Home Affairs or any other law \nenforcement agency/intelligence structure is contemplated against the members \nof the ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e):     A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n--2--   \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *132 FOR \nANSWER ON 11.02.2020 REGARDING “TUKDE TUKDE GANG” \n \n(a) to (e): No such information has been brought to the notice of the \nGovernment by any law enforcement agency.  \n******* \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_133": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.133 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nITIs \n \n133.  SHRI MAGUNTASREENIVASULU REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the Industrial Training Institu tes (ITIs) functioning in the country at \npresent along with the skill trades being taught in  these institutes, State/UT-wise \nincluding Andhra Pradesh; \n \n(b)  the total estimated number of students getting  training in these institutes and the \nexisting vacancies in various categories along with  the plan to fill up these vacancies, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to set up new ITIs in the country and if so,the \ndetails thereof, State/phase-wise; \n \n(d)   whether the Ministry has prepared any fresh p lan to upgrade ITIs in the country \nand if so, the details thereof including integratio n of ITIs with skill development \nprogrammes; and \n \n(e)  the details of the amount spent on improvement  of ITIs in the country during each \nof the last three years and the current year, State-wise? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (b) There are 15697  Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs) functioning in the \ncountry at present and imparting training in 137 sk ill trades. The detail of State/UT wise \nITIs, including the state of Andhra Pradesh, is att ached at Annexure I and list of 137 \ntrades is at Annexure II. \n \nAround 21 lakh trainees are undergoing training aga inst the total seating capacity \nof 26.65 lakh in various trades categories. Admissi on in ITIs is under the administrative \ncontrol of concerned State Directorates and state w ise rounds of admissions are \nconducted by them to fill vacancies in various categories. \n \n \n \n \n\n(c) Setting up of Industrial Training Institutes (I TIs) and its operations are in the \ndomain of respective State/UT Governments. \n \n (d)  Yes sir , top grade ITIs, in the country including their inte gration with skill \ndevelopment program, a World Bank funded scheme Skills Strengthening for \nIndustrial Value Enhancement (STRIVE)  has been approved by Cabinet Committee for \nEconomic Affairs (CCEA). It is a central sector sch eme launched in October 2017 for a \ntotal cost of Rs. 2200 crore, and 5 years duration;  with an objective to improve relevance \nand efficiency of skills development provided throu gh ITIs and apprenticeship. The \nscheme implemented with effect from financial year 2018-19, has following four \ncomponents:  \nS.No. \n Component \n1 To Improve Performance of ITIs \n2 To Increase Capacities of State Governments to su pport and \nmanage ITIs and apprenticeship training  \n3 To Improve Teaching and Learning  \n4 To Improve and Broadened Apprenticeship Training \n \n(e)  The details of the amount spent on improvement of I TIs in the country during the \nlast three years and the current year, State-wise, under different schemes as given \nbelow, is attached in annexure III, IV& V respectively. \n1. World Bank assisted Vocational Training Improvement  Project (VTIP):  The \nscheme, interalia covers up-gradation of 400 Govern ment ITIs have been \ncovered in 34 States/UTs. The scheme was closed on September 2018 . \n2. Skills Strengthening for Industrial Value Enhanceme nt (STRIVE) is a new \ncentral sector World Bank funded project at a total  cost of Rs. 2200 crore. The \nproject duration is 5 years and the closing date is November 2022.  \n3. Upgradation of existing Govt. ITIs into Model ITIs:  - Under this scheme, the \nfunds were released for improvement of Govt. ITIs. \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply of part (a) to (b) of  LSUQ No.133 on ITIs raised by \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY for answer on 03.02.2020  \n \nSTATE/UT WISE DETAILS \nSl. No  \n \nName of State/UT \n \nNumber of ITIs \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS 4 \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH  513  \n3 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 6 \n4 ASSAM 37 \n5 BIHAR 1264 \n6 CHANDIGARH 2 \n7 CHHATTISGARH 235 \n8 DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI 1 \n9 DAMAN AND DIU 2 \n10 DELHI 60 \n11 GOA 15 \n12 GUJARAT 530 \n13 HARYANA 405 \n14 HIMACHAL PRADESH 292 \n15 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 41 \n16 JHARKHAND 327 \n17 KARNATAKA 1557 \n18 KERALA 651 \n19 LAKSHADWEEP 1 \n20 MADHYA PRADESH 1167 \n21 MAHARASHTRA 994 \n22 MANIPUR 1 \n23 MEGHALAYA 6 \n24 MIZORAM 3 \n25 NAGALAND 2 \n26 ODISHA 580 \n27 PUDUCHERRY 15 \n28 PUNJAB 384 \n29 RAJASTHAN 1923 \n30 SIKKIM 3 \n31 TAMIL NADU 592 \n32 TELANGANA 302 \n33 TRIPURA 20 \n34 UTTAR PRADESH 3302 \n35 UTTARAKHAND 195 \n36 WEST BENGAL 265 \nTotal 15697 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II  \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply of part (a) to (b) of  LSUQ No.133 on ITIs raised by \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY for answer on 03.02.2020  \n \nTotal no. of Trades: 137 \nList of trades (Engineering, Non Engineering & Divy ang) under Craftsmen Training \nScheme (CTS) \nEngineering Trades: 73 \nSl. No. \n Name of the Trade \n1 Electrician \n2 Electronics Mechanic \n3 Fitter \n4 Turner \n5 Welder \n6 Draughtsman (Civil) \n7 Carpenter \n8 Draughtsman Mechanical \n9 Domestic Painter \n10 Electroplater \n11 Foundryman \n12 Industrial Painter \n13 Instrument Mechanic \n14 Interior Design and Decoration \n15 Machinist Grinder \n16 Machinist \n17 Marine Engine Fitter \n18 Marine Fitter \n19 Mechanic Consumer Electronic Appliances \n20 Mechanic Two and Three Wheeler \n21 Mechanic Auto Body Painting \n22 Mechanic Auto Body Repair \n23 Mechanic Refrigeration and Air Conditioner \n24 Mechanic Agricultural Machinery \n25 Mechanic Auto Electrical and Electronics \n26 Mechanic Diesel \n27 Mechanic Tractor \n28 Mechanic Machine Tool Maintenance \n29 Operator Advance Machine Tool \n30 Painter (General) \n31 Plastic Processing Operator \n32 Pump Operator cum Mechanic \n33 Radiology Technician \n34 Sheet Metal Worker \n35 Solar Technician (Electrical) \n36 Surveyor \n37 Tool & Die Maker (Dies &Moulds) \n38 Tool & Die Maker (Press Tools, Jigs & Fixtures) \n39 Technician Medical Electronics \n40 Technician Mechatronics \n41 Technician Power Electronics Systems \n42 Vessel Navigator \n43 Welder (GMAW & GTAW) \n44 Welder (Pipe) \n45 Welder (Structural) \n\n46 Welder (Fabrication & Fitting) \n47 Welder (Welding & Inspection) \n48 Wireman \n49 Attendant Operator (Chemical Plant) \n50 Instrument Mechanic (Chemical Plant) \n51 Laboratory Assistant (Chemical Plant) \n52 Maintenance Mechanic (Chemical Plant) \n53 Mechanic Motor Vehicle \n54 Architectural Draughtsman \n55 Central Air Condition Plant Mechanic \n56 Civil Engineering Assistant \n57 Information and Communication Technology System Maintenance \n58 Information Technology \n59 Lift and Escalator Mechanic \n60 Mason (Building Constructor) \n61 Mechanic Lens/ Prism Grinding \n62 Mechanic Mining Machinery \n63 Plumber \n64 Rubber Technician \n65 Spinning Technician \n66 Textile Wet Processing Technician \n67 Weaving Technician \n68 Refractory Technician \n69 Stone Processing Machine Operator \n70 Stone Mining Machine Operator \n71 Textile Mechatronics \n72 Additive Manufacturing Technician (3D Printing) \n73 Aeronautical Structure and Equipment Fitter \nList of Non Engineering Trades: 59 \nSl. No.  Name of the Trade \n1. Remotely Piloted Aircraft (RPA)/Drone Pilot \n2. Floriculture & Landscaping \n3. Horticulture \n4. Soil Testing and Crop Technician \n5. Computer Aided Embroidery & Designing \n6. Dress Making \n7. Fashion Design and Technology \n8. Sewing Technology \n9. Driver cum Mechanic \n10. Basic Cosmetology \n11. Spa Therapy \n12. Finance Executive \n13. Early Childhood Educator \n14. IoT Technician (Smart Agriculture) \n15. IoT Technician (Smart City) \n16. IoT Technician (Smart Health Care) \n17. Smartphone Technician Cum App Tester \n18. Baker & Confectioner \n19. Dairying \n20. Agro Processing \n21. Food and Beverage Service Assistant \n22. Food Beverages \n23. Fruits and Vegetables Processing \n24. Milk and Milk Product Technician \n25. Marketing Executive ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_134": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 134 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nSchool Textbooks \n \n134. SHRI PINAKI MISRA: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Paika  Bidroha of 1817 has been included in the school history \ntextbooks as the first war of independence against British Rule; \n \n(b) whether there are plans to popularise the rebellion in schools through other \nmethods like workshops, plays, competitions, or lectures; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL 'NISHANK') \n \n(a) The ‘Paika Bidroha’ has been mentioned under the heading `The Khurda \nUprising – A case Study` in the National Council of Educational Research and \nTraining (NCERT)’s Social Science textbook , namely, “Our Pasts -III” (Part-II) for \nClass VIII. \n \n(b) No such proposal is under consideration at present. \n \n(c)  Does not arise. \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_135": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n135\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE   \n11\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 20\n20\n \n \nFARMERS REGISTERED UNDER PM\n-\nKISAN SCHEME\n \n \n*135. \n \nSHRI ARVIND GANPAT SAWANT: \n \nSHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe number of farmers registered under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi \nYojana\n \n(PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme) and the number of farmers benefited, out of them, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nthe details of funds allocated for the said scheme and the total amount being disbursed \nto the beneficiaries; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether\n \nit is true that all the enrolled farmers are not getting the benefit under the above \nscheme and if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n \n(d) \n \nthe manner in which the Ministry ensures that only genuine farmers are getting \nbenefited under the scheme; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nwheth\ner the Government has a mechanism to check whether the said scheme has \nbrought any major changes in the lives of beneficiaries, if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (e):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  135   DUE FOR REPLY ON 11\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY, 2020.\n \n \n(a):\n \nAs on 06.02.2020, a total of \n96,073,451\n \nfarmer families have been successfully \nregistered under PM\n-\nKISAN out of which 84,472,629 beneficiaries have received benefits \nunder the Scheme. The State\n-\nwise details of the same are given at Annexure\n-\n1.\n \n \n(b):\n \nBudget\nary allocation of Rs. 20,000 Crore and Rs. 75,000 Crore have been provided in \nFinancial Year 2018\n-\n19 & 2019\n-\n20 respectively for the implementation of PM\n-\nKISAN \nScheme, out of which, as of 06.02.2020, an amount of Rs. 50522.2 Crore has been \ntransferred in th\ne bank account\ns\n \nof beneficiaries across the country since inception of the \nScheme.\n \n(c)\n:\n     \nPM\n-\nKISAN is a continuous and ongoing Scheme, in which the financial benefits are \ntransferred to the bank accounts of the identified beneficiaries as and when their \ncorrect and \nverified data is uploaded by the concerned States / UTs on PM\n-\nKisan web\n-\nportal.  The data \nof beneficiaries so uploaded by the concerned States/UTs undergoes a multi\n-\nlevel \nverification and validation by various concerned agencies before making p\nayment to the \nbeneficiaries\n, hence, merely registration on the Portal does not ensure ben\nefit transfer to the \nbeneficiaries\n.\n \n(d)\n:\n \n \nIn order to release PM\n-\nKISAN benefits, Ministry receives verified details i.e. details of \nland holdings, villages, bank relat\ned details, Aadhar car\nd number etc. of the beneficiaries\n \nfrom \nthe concerned States/UTs. These details are required to verified by the PM\n-\nKISAN \nPortal, initially the account validation is done through Public Finance Management System \n(PFMS). From December, \n2019 onwards AADH\nAR authentication of beneficiaries\n \ndata is \nalso made mandatory. In case, any mismatch of provided details has been noticed by the \nabove \n  \nmentioned \n \nagencies, \n   \ndetails related\n  \n \nto concerned beneficiaries are required to be \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n\n \ncorrected or amen\nded by the concerned States/UTs.  For this purpose\n \ndetails of concerned \nbeneficiaries are made available to the concerned States/UTs. This way, Ministry ensures \nben\nefit transfer to the beneficiaries\n \nonly on the basis of verification of the\n \nconcerned State/UT \nGovernments. In the cases of self\n-\nregistration through the Farmers Corner in the portal, \nMinistry seeks \nverified data of the beneficiaries\n \nfrom the concerned Stat\ne\n/UT Governments \nbefore transfer the benefit under PM\n-\nKISAN. \n \n(e)\n:\n \nAgricul\nture is a State subject. The prime responsibility of development of Agriculture \nrests with the concerned State/UT Governments. Government of India, however, through its \nvarious Schemes/Missions, supplements the efforts of the State/UT Gove\nrnments for the \nd\nevelopment of a\ngriculture and welfare of the farmers. With a view to provide income support \nto all landholding farmer families across the country, to enable them to take care of expenses \nrelated to agricultural & allied activities and to save them from the\n \nclutches of the \nmoneylenders, PM\n-\nK\nISAN was launched. The Scheme was\n \nlaunched on 24.02.2019 a\nnd the \nsame is in nascent state.  As such\n, \nno\n \nformal study of the Scheme has been done yet.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\n1   \n \n \nState wise details of farmers registered and benefitted under PM\n-\nKISAN \n \n(as on 06/02/2020)\n \nState/UT\n \nNo. of Farmers\n \nSuccessfully\n \nRegistered under\n \nPM\n-\nKISAN\n \nFarmers benefitted\n \nunder PM\n-\nKISAN\n \nANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS\n \n16,830\n \n16,504\n \nANDHRA \nPRADESH\n \n5,445,035\n \n5,117,781\n \nARUNACHAL PRADESH\n \n63,173\n \n50,820\n \nASSAM\n \n3,665,983\n \n2,704,199\n \nBIHAR\n \n5,757,826\n \n5,348,465\n \nCHANDIGARH\n \n462\n \n457\n \nCHHATTISGARH\n \n2,15\n3,461\n \n1,880,819\n \nDADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI\n \n10,586\n \n10,435\n \nDAMAN AND DIU\n \n3,664\n \n3,463\n \nDELHI\n \n14,304\n \n12,479\n \nGOA\n \n8,677\n \n7,248\n \nGUJARAT\n \n5,128,406\n \n4,825,858\n \nHARYANA\n \n1,648,596\n \n1,445,519\n \nHIMACHAL PRADESH\n \n891,948\n \n863,890\n \nJAMMU AND KASHMIR\n \n1,024,262\n \n930,559\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n1,763,965\n \n1,450,215\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n5,081,526\n \n4,905,076\n \nKERALA\n \n2,998,384\n \n2,750,074\n \nLAKSHADW\nEEP\n \n1,699\n \n-\n \nMADHYA PRADESH\n \n6,618,019\n \n5,513,954\n \nMAHARASHTRA\n \n9,476,776\n \n8,457,133\n \nMANIPUR\n \n224,129\n \n173,789\n \nMEGHALAYA\n \n78,331\n \n70,236\n \nMIZORAM\n \n79,460\n \n67,534\n \nNAGALAND\n \n183,760\n \n168,749\n \nODISHA\n \n3,792,320\n \n3,628,657\n \nPUDUCHERRY\n \n9,919\n \n9,503\n \nPUNJAB\n \n2,371,\n389\n \n2,240,064\n \nRAJASTHAN\n \n6,254,316\n \n5,204,512\n \nSIKKIM\n \n11,760\n \n11\n \nTAMIL NADU\n \n3,709,674\n \n3,502,323\n \nTELANGANA\n \n3,629,527\n \n3,480,436\n \nTRIPURA\n \n200,245\n \n194,695\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n23,003,675\n \n18,735,405\n \nUTTARAKHAND\n \n7\n51,364\n \n701,767\n \nWEST BENGAL\n \n-\n \n-\n \nTotal:\n \n96,073,451\n \n84,472,629\n \n \n******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_136": "�\n�\n������������������� �\n����������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n����������������������� � �� �\n����������� ��������� � ����������� ������ �\n������� ������������� �\n�\n�\n������������������� ��\n�������� �\n� �� �������������������� ���\n�������������������������������������� �������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� ��\n�\n������������������������������ ������������������������������������������� �����������\n��������������������������� ������������������������������������������ ��������� �\n�\n���������������������������������� ���������������������������������� ������������������\n������������������������ ����������������������������������������������� ��������������������\n��� �\n�\n���� �������� ���� ������� ���� ���� � ������������ ��� �������� ���� ����� ������ � ������\n��������������������������������� �������������������������\n�\n������ �\n������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������� ������������������������������������������������������ �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ����\n���� ��� �������������� ���� ��� ���� ���������� ����� ����� ������ ��������� ������ ��������\n�������������������������������������������������� ���������������������� �����������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������� ������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������� ����������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������ �������������\n����������� ���������������������������������������� �