� �� ������ ��\n���������� ��������� �������� �\n����� �\n��� � �������� � ��������\n������� �\n������������ ��� ��������\n���������������� ���\n�������������� �������\n��������� �����������\n������������������������ �\n����� �\n��� � ���������� � ��������\n������� �\n��������������������������\n������� ���� ��� �������\n���������� ������ ������ ���\n�������� ���� ����������\n��� ��������� �\n����� �\n�\n��� � ������� � �������������\n��������\n������������ ��� ��������\n�������� �������� ������� �\n������ ��� ���� �� ���� �\n�������� ������� ��\n������� �� �������� �\n�������� ���������������\n����� �\n�\n��� � ��������\n������� �\n���������\n��������\n������������ ��� �������� ���\n������� ��� ���������� ���\n����� �\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_52": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 52 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 05th  FEBRUARY, 2020 \n \nMULTILINGUAL APP OF THE SUPREME COURT \n \n†*52 SHRI ANIL FIROJIYA: \nSHRI MOHANBHAI KAYANJI KUNDARIYA : \n \n            Will the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \na) the time by which the new app ‘Supreme Court Vidhik Anuvaad Software’ \nlaunched by the Supreme Court of India is likely to become functional;  \nb) the number of languages in whic h the app is going to provide updates \nabout the legal proceedings;  \nc) whether the provisions are also in place to make the information \naccessible to the visually impaired;  \nd) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \ne) whether any other n ew digitization initiative is being taken to increase the \naccess to legal information by all the citizens of the country including print \ndisabled citizens in near future and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION No. 52 DUE FOR ANSWER ON \n05.02.2020. \n(a) With a view to enable common people of India to understand the \njudgments of Supreme Court in easy manner in local languages , \nSUVAS (Supreme Court Vidhik Anuvaa d Software) has been \ndeveloped as a path breaking initiative by the Supreme Court of India \nin collabor ation and with  technical support from the Ministry of \nElectronics and Information Technology, Government of India  aided \nby experts from IIT and IIIT.  \nThis pilot development of SUVAS is at present in testing, training and \nrefinement phase in 18 High Courts in the country. The work covers \ntranslating judgments related to cases arising under the appellate \njurisdiction of the Supreme Court of India in relation to the following \nsubject categories: \n1. Labour matters; \n2. Rent Act matters; \n3. Land Acquisition and Requisition matters; \n4. Service matters; \n5. Compensation matters; \n6. Criminal matters; \n7. Family Law matters; \n8. Ordinary Civil matters; \n9. Personal Law matters; \n10. Religious and Charitable Endowments matters; \n11. Simple money and Mortgage matters; \n12. Eviction under the Public Premises(Eviction) Act matters; \n13. Land Laws and Agriculture Tenancies; and \n\n14. Matters relating to Consumer Protection. \n \nThe translation is functional from English language into Hindi, Bengali, \nGujarati, Kannada, Malayalam, Marathi, Punjabi, Tamil and Telugu \nlanguages. \nOnce the pilot phase of testing, training and refinement, as mentioned \nabove, is completed, and the facility becomes functional, it would be rolled \nout fully in the relevant High Courts. \n(b) The Official Multilingual Mobile App of Supreme Court of I ndia, provides \nupdates about the legal proceedings in six languages i.e. English, Hindi, \nKannada, Marathi, Tamil and Telugu.  \n(c) & (d)  At present the Official Mobile App of Supreme Court is not separately \nequipped with accessibility of information to visually impaired. \n(e) Through computerization of 16,845 District & Subordinate Courts and ICT \nenablement under the eCourts Project Phase -II, several services have \nbeen provided to litigants, lawyers and Judiciary which facilitate \nexpeditious delivery of judicial  services. eCourts services such as details \nof case registration, cause list, case status, daily orders & final judgments \nare available to litigants and advocates through eCourts web portal, \nJudicial Service Centers (JSC) in all computerized courts, eCourt s Mobile \nApp, email service, SMS push & pull services. Integration of eCourts \nServices through Common Service Centers (CSCs) has been successfully \ncompleted. eCourt CNR Service has been enabled through Digital Seva \nPortal in all CSC locations across the co untry. National Judicial Data Grid \n(NJDG) for District & Subordinate Courts, created as an online platform \nunder the Project, provides information relating to judicial \nproceedings/decisions of computerized district and subordinate courts of \n\nthe country. Cu rrently, all stakeholders including Judicial Officers can \naccess case status information in respect of over 12.97 crore pending and \ndisposed cases and more than 11.15 crore orders / judgments pertaining \nto these computerized courts. Video Conferencing faci lity has been \nenabled between 3240 court complexes & 1272 corresponding jails. \n…….. \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_53": " \n \n1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 53  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH FEBRUARY, 2020 \n \nBHARATNET PROJECT \n \n*53. SHRI UTTAM KUMAR REDDY NALAMADA:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the funds allocated under the BharatNet Project since its inception, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(b)  the length of optical fibre cable laid down under the said project, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(c)  the number of Gram Panchayats that have been provided with broadband connectivity under \nthe scheme till date, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(d)  the number of functional Wi-Fi hotspots in the country, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(e)  the reasons for delay in achieving the targets under this scheme; and  \n \n(f)  the time by which the Government proposes to achieve the target of providing broadband \nconnectivity to all the 2.5 lakh Gram Panchayats? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS, LAW & JUSTICE AND  \nELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n \n(a) to (f)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \n2 \n \nSTATEMENT TO BE LAID ON THE TABLE OF LOK SABHA IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) \nto (f) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. * 53 FOR 5TH FEBRUARY, 2020   \nREGARDING “BHARATNET PROJECT ”. \n \n(a) BharatNet project is being implemented in a phased manner to provide broadband \nconnectivity to all the Gram Panchayats (approx. 2,50,000) in the country.  Funds for the BharatNet \nproject are allocated as a whole and not State/Union Territory-wise. A lump sum amount is allocated \nand disbursed from Universal Service Obligation Fund to Bharat Broadband Network Limited for \nexecution of BharatNet project. The details of State/UT wise funds  disbursed/allocated by Bharat \nBroadband Network Limited under BharatNet Project, are given in Annex-I. \n(b) to (d) The State/UT -wise details of length o f optical fibre cable laid, the  number of Gram \nPanchayats that have been provided with broadband connectivity, and the number of functional Wi-Fi \nhotspots in the country under the scheme are given in Annex-II. \n(e) BharatNet is a project of mega nature and the GPs are widely dispersed across the country in \nrural and remote areas. All out efforts are being made to expeditiously implement the project. The \ndelay in the implementation of the project is mainly on account of: \n(i) Delay in the initial commencement of the project as the work on the project began in the \nsecond half of 2014 due to field survey of GPs and pilot testing of the technology model.   \n(ii) In Phase-I, only one media that is underground OFC to cover GPs, and Right of Way \n(RoW) issues. \n(iii)  Under Phase-II, about 65,000 GPs  in 8 States are being implemented under State -led \nmodel, and delay in their implementation is also adversely affecting the completion of  \nthe Project.  \n(f) The broadband connectivity to all the 2.5 lakh Gram Panchayats is targeted to be provided by \nAugust 2021. \n  \n\n \n \n3 \n \nAnnex-I \nAnnexure referred to in Part (a) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *53 for Answer on \n05.02.2020. \nDetails of funds disbursed under BharatNet Project Ph-I &Ph-II (BBNL) \nS. No. State/Union Territory  Funds Disbursed (In \nCrore) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 15.22 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 345.01 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 45.58 \n4 Assam 185.82 \n5 Bihar 715.71 \n6 Chandigarh  0.01 \n7 Chhattisgarh 941.36 \n8 Gujarat, Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 1607.20 \n9 Haryana 414.27 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 110.58 \n11 Jammu & Kashmir 79.53 \n12 Jharkhand 352.28 \n13 Karnataka 782.65 \n14 Kerala 103.39 \n15 Lakshadweep 0.10 \n16 Madhya Pradesh 1471.93 \n17 Maharashtra 1932.85 \n18 Manipur 50.55 \n19 Meghalaya 66.97 \n20 Mizoram 34.25 \n21 Nagaland 60.07 \n22 Odisha 546.79 \n23 Puducherry 5.70 \n24 Punjab 625.41 \n25 Rajasthan 889.76 \n26 Sikkim 76.33 \n27 Tamil Nadu 121.46 \n28 Telangana 336.10 \n29 Tripura 75.70 \n30 Uttar Pradesh 1624.03 \n31 Uttarakhand 266.30 \n32 West Bengal 430.24 \nTotal 14313.11 \nGPON & OFC 976.10 \nAd hoc payment done for BSNL 4209.89 \nTCIL 13.43 \nBSNL-VSAT 42.38 \nBSNL Satellite Band width 40.12 \nGrand Total 19595.03 \n \n \n \n\n \n \n4 \n \nAnnex-II \nAnnexure referred to in Part (b) to (d) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *53 for Answer \non 05.02.2020. \nStatus of BharatNet connectivity \nSr. \nNo.  \nStates/Union Territory  Cable \nlaid (km) \nGPs for \nwhich \nCable \nlaid \nService \nReady \nGPs \n(Includin\ng BHQs) \nNo. of \nGPs \nwhere   \nWi-Fi \nhotspots \ninstalled \nNo. of \nGPs \nwhere   \nWi-Fi \nhotspots \nactive \n1 Andhra Pradesh 18771 4364 1628 0 0 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 1519 579 244 160 155 \n3 Andaman & Nicobar  34 26 8 0 0 \n4 Assam 4799 1622 1622 4 4 \n5 Bihar 26232 8366 8067 292 219 \n6 Chandigarh 19 13 13 12 12 \n7 Chhattisgarh 15524 4727 4712 517 435 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 68 21 21 0 0 \n9 Daman & Diu 26 18 18 0 0 \n10 Gujarat 36936 10887 10545 86 65 \n11 Haryana 11930 6188 6188 1063 712 \n12 Himachal Pradesh 766 250 281 200 157 \n13 \nJammu & Kashmir and \nLadakh 766 374 1011 740 661 \n14 Jharkhand 8405 2750 2478 312 254 \n15 Karnataka 14178 6244 6244 2569 1869 \n16 Kerala 830 1129 1129 731 728 \n17 Lakshadweep 0 0 0 0 0 \n18 Madhya Pradesh 48365 13666 12922 1350 826 \n19 Maharashtra 54153 18838 15534 776 437 \n20 Manipur 634 376 427 204 44 \n21 Mizoram 689 130 66 60 20 \n22 Meghalaya 1069 356 194 70 70 \n23 Nagaland 2000 656 129 0 0 \n24 Odisha 17472 4395 3892 113 107 \n25 Puducherry 92 101 101 81 81 \n26 Punjab 24003 12536 12529 0 0 \n27 Rajasthan 29208 8766 8724 8813 3964 \n28 Sikkim 855 41 18 0 0 \n29 Tamil Nadu 0 0 0 0 0 \n30 Telangana 4772 2047 2047 0 0 \n31 Tripura 1687 817 647 157 77 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 70950 32411 28998 27095 6827 \n33 Uttarakhand 3583 1529 1526 353 315 \n34 West Bengal 8591 2494 2285 11 2 \nTotal 408926 146717 134248 45769 18041 \n************ ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_54": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 54 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n \nABSENCE OF PROPER LIGHTING AT STATIONS \n \n †*54. SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that passengers face \ndifficulties in identifying the station in darkness due to lack of \nproper lighting at railway stations located in rural areas; \n \n(b) whether passengers also face difficulties due to the absence of \nan announcement system in the trains about the next station; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to make arrangements for \nthe advance announcement of name of the next station as well \nas written information in each coach thro ugh screening board \non the lines of metro trains to address the said problem; and \n \n(d) if so, the time by which it is likely to be done? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n \n  (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n  \n(a)  to (d):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 54 BY SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 05.02.2020 REGARDING ABSENCE OF \nPROPER LIGHTING AT STATIONS \n \n(a): Based on suggestion received from Consultative Committee of MPs \nfor the Ministry of Railways  for need of proper illumination of station \nname boards displayed at the two ends of  the platforms, it was \nreiterated to Zonal Railways that electric poles provided at the \nplatforms of all electrified stations should be  adjusted in such a \nmanner that station name boards provided at the two ends of the \nplatforms are properly illuminated so as to be read easily during night. \nIt was also emphasized  that lights specifically provid ed for \nillumination of the station name boards are kept glowing during the \nnight. So far, 8016 stations name board are illuminated during night \ntime. \n(b) to (d) : Suggestion has been received from Member of Consultative \nCommittee that coaches should have fa cilities of display of name of \napproaching stations.  \nIn the existing coach design, next station name announcement is \nprovided on some of the trains like Electrical Multiple Units \n(EMUs)/Mainline Electric Multiple Units (MEMUs) suburban trains and \nother select group of trains like Rajdhani, Shtabdi, Tejas and Humsafar \netc. \nContd.2/- \n\n \n \n-2- \nFurther, Modern Coach Factory (MCF) and Rail Coach Factory (RCF) \nare a dvised for fitment of system as per Research Designs and \nStandards Organization (RDSO)  trial specification for field trial on 50 \ncoaches each by MCF and RCF and fitment is under process.  All newly \nmanufactured EMU/MEMU rakes are already equipped with such \ntechnology/system. \nTo further proliferate this system, specification of Public Address \nand Passenger Information System (PAPIS) has been issued in August \n2019. Display of name of approaching station is part of PAPIS.  Initially \nfitment of PAPIS for field trial has been started in 100 coaches and will \nbe completed on priority.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_55": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 55 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nLiteracy Rate  \n†55.  SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the literacy rate has improved in the country during the recent years;  \n(b) if so, the details of the literacy rate during the last three years and the current year, \nState/UT-wise;  \n(c) the separate percentage of persons who have pursued education upto primary, upper \nprimary, secondary, higher secondary and graduation and post graduation;  \n(d) whether any effort have been made/proposed to be made to increase the literacy rate in the \ncountry; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL 'NISHANK') \n \n(a) & (b) The literacy data in the country is collected through decennial census operation \nconducted by Registrar General of India. The last Census was held in 2011.  Census 2011 \nrecorded literacy rate as 7 2.98 per cent compared to 64.8 4 in 2001 and 52.2 1 per cent in \n1991. A statement indicating State/UT and gender -wise literacy rate in the country in the age \ngroup of 7 and above, as per Census 2011 is at Annexure-I. \n \n(c) As per census 2011, separate percentage of persons who have pursued education upto \nPrimary, Secondary, Higher Secondary and Graduation & above is given at Annexure-II. \n(d) & (e) In order to improve the literacy rate in the country, the scheme of Saakshar Bharat \nwas implemented in the rural areas of 410 districts in 26 States and one UT, th at had adult \nfemale literacy rate of 50 per cent and below as per Census 2001, with special focus on \nwomen and other disadvantaged groups.  \n\nIn addition, the Department of School Education and Literacy has launched the \nSamagra Shiksha - an Integrated Scheme  for School Education as a Centrally Sponsored \nScheme with effect from the year 2018 -19. This programme subsumes the three erstwhile \nCentrally Sponsored Schemes of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA), Rashtriya Madhyamik \nShiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) and Teacher Educatio n (TE). Samagra Shiksha is an overarching \nprogramme for the school education sector extending from pre -school to class XII and aims \nto ensure inclusive and equitable quality education at all levels of school education. \n**** \n\nAnnexure-I \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 55 FOR 03.02.2020 ASKED BY SHRI RAVINDRA \nKUSHWAHA REGARDING “LITERACY RATE”. \nState/UT and gender -wise literacy rate in the country in the age group of 7 and above, \nas per census 2011 \n \n \n  \nS. No. Name of States / UTs \nLiteracy Rate \nPersons Male Female \nIndia 72.98 80.88 64.63 \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 67.02 74.88 59.15 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh 65.38 72.55 57.70 \n3.  Assam 72.19 77.85 66.27 \n4.  Bihar 61.80 71.20 51.50 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 70.28 80.27 60.24 \n6.  Goa 88.70 92.65 84.66 \n7.  Gujarat 78.03 85.75 69.68 \n8.  Haryana 75.55 84.06 65.94 \n9.  Himachal Pradesh 82.80 89.53 75.93 \n10.  Jammu & Kashmir 67.16 76.75 56.43 \n11.  Jharkhand 66.41 76.84 55.42 \n12.  Karnataka 75.36 82.47 68.08 \n13.  Kerala 94.00 96.11 92.07 \n14.  Madhya Pradesh 69.32 78.73 59.24 \n15.  Maharashtra 82.34 88.38 75.87 \n16.  Manipur 76.94 83.58  70.26  \n17.  Meghalaya 74.43 75.95 72.89 \n18.  Mizoram 91.33 93.35 89.27 \n19.  Nagaland 79.55 82.75 76.11 \n20.  Odisha 72.87 81.59 64.01 \n21.  Punjab 75.84 80.44 70.73 \n22.  Rajasthan 66.11 79.19 52.12 \n23.  Sikkim 81.42 86.55 75.61 \n24.  Tamil Nadu 80.09 86.77 73.44 \n25.  Tripura 87.22 91.53 82.73 \n26.  Uttar Pradesh 67.68 77.28 57.18 \n27.  Uttarakhand 78.82 87.40 70.01 \n28.  West Bengal 76.26 81.69 70.54 \n29.  A & N Islands 86.63 90.27 82.43 \n30.  Chandigarh 86.05 89.99 81.19 \n31.  D & N Haveli 76.24 85.17 64.32 \n32.  Daman & Diu 87.10 91.54 79.55 \n33.  Lakshadweep 91.85 95.56 87.95 \n34.  NCT of Delhi 86.21 90.94 80.76 \n35.  Puducherry 85.85 91.26 80.67 \n\nAnnexure-II \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 55 FOR 03.02.2020 ASKED BY SHRI RAVINDRA \nKUSHWAHA REGARDING “LITERACY RATE”. \nPercentage of persons who have pursued education upto Primary, Secondary, Higher \nSecondary and Graduation & above as per Census 2011 \n \nTotal Population \n(in crore) \n(7 years & above) \nEducational Level Population \n(in crore) \nPercentage \n(7+ age group) \n \n104.63 \nUp to Primary \n(up to class 7) \n33.11 31.64 \nMiddle \n(class 8 to 9) \n13.39 12.80 \nSecondary \n(class 10 to 11) \n10.59 10.12 \nHigher Secondary \n(class 12 to 2nd year of \nGraduation) \n7.79 7.44 \nGraduation & above 6.83 6.53 \n \n1. The above information is based on RGI Report of census 2011. \n2. As per RGI report, the tabulation categories are as under:  \nTabulation Categories \n03 Below Primary \n04 4th to 7th (Primary) \n05 8th to 9th (Middle) \n06 10th to 11th (Secondary) \n07 12th to 2nd Yeary of BA/BSc/BCom (Higher Secondary) \n24 and above  Graduation and above \n \n3. Upto Primary includes below Primary and Primary as per RGI report of Census 2011. \n4. Separate percentage for Graduate & above given in the table at Annexure-II as the Registrar General of \nIndia has not provided the separate data for Graduate and Post-Graduate. \n*** ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_56": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n                                   \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 56 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n  \nCOAL MINES OPERATING WITHOUT CLEARANCES \n*56. SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n(a) whether any mines of Coal India Limited (CIL), Central Coalfields Limited (CCL) or their \nsubsidiaries are operating without valid Environment Clearance, Consent to Establish and \nConsent to Operate; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, mine-wise; \n(c) the reasons for not procuring the required certificates from the concerned authorities; \n(d) whether any action has been taken against CCL or its subsidiaries by the respective State \nPollution Control Boards, State Environment Ministries, Ministry of Environment, Forest and \nClimate Change (MoEFCC) or any Central or State Government body including the State \ndepartment of mines and if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether any monetary penalties or cess have been imposed on CCL or its subsidiaries for \noperating mines without proper permissions since the year 2014; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof along with the amount of penalties imposed on CCL and its \nsubsidiaries, year-wise? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES  \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI)  \n  \n(a) to (f): A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n  \n  \n\n  \n16th Position \nStatement r efers to r eply part ( a) to ( f) in r espect of Lok Sabh a Q uestion No. * 56 for \n05.02.2020 asked by Shri Sushil Kumar Singh  regarding ��Coal Mines Operating without \n������������������������ \n(a) to (c):  As informed by Coal India Limited (CIL), no mines of CIL, Central Coalfields \nLimited (CCL) or its subsidiaries is operating without valid Environment Clearance (EC), \nConsent to Establish (CTE) and Consent to Operate (CTO).  \n  \nHowever for some of the mines of CCL, where Environmental Clearance (EC)/ Consent to \nOperate (CTO) were withdrawn, application for EC/ CTO have been submitted to concerned \nagencies.    \n \n(d) to (f):   As informed by CIL, for CCL, following action has been taken by Jharkhand State \nPollution Control Board (JSPCB) and state department of mines:  \n1. CTO of 4 projects revoked by JSPCB on 20.01.2020. \n2. Credible action against project officials of Karo Open Cast Co al Mine, Selected Dhori \nOpen Cast Coal Mine & Tarmi Open Cast Coal Mine has been taken by JSPCB. \n3. Demand notices issued by District Mining Officers of Jharkhand from CCL for \nRs.13,568.504 Crore in view of common cause judgment dated 02.08.2017. The demand \nnotices have been stayed by Revisional Authority, Ministry of Coal. \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_57": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION N\nO. 57\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY 3\nRD\n \nJANUARY, 2020 MAGHA 14\n, 1941 (SAKA)\n \nDIGITAL TAXATION REGIME\n \n57.  SHRIMATI KANIMO\nZHI KARUNANIDHI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nFINANCE \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether the Government is considering to bring Digital Taxation regime in India to tax the internet  \ncompanies and MNCs;\n \n(b)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof; and\n \n(c)\n \n \nif not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN\n \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\n \n \n(SHRI ANURAG\n \nSINGH\n \nTHAKUR)\n \n \n(a), (b) and (c)\n: Several proposals pertaining to direct taxes are examined during the budgetary exercise \nfor the Union Budget, during which the Finance Bill is tabled before the Parliament and the proposal\ns \nwhich are finalised are reflected in the Finance Bill which is being presented every year in Lok Sabha \non 1\nst\n \nFebruary since 2017.\n \n------\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_58": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 58 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03rd February, 2020 \nAuction of Oil Fields in AP \n \n58. SHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \n \nisVªksfy;e ,oa izkd``frd xSl ea=h \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether 16 oil fields in and around Rajahmundry in Andhra Pradesh (AP) ar e going to \nbe auctioned by ONGC; \n(b) if so, the details of fields that are going to be auctioned and the anticipated output from \nthese fields;  \n(c) the time by which the entire bid process is going to be completed and fields become \noperational; and \n(d) the details of benefits likely to be accrued particularly to the city of Rajahmundry in \nparticular and the district in general? \nANSWER \n \nisVªksfy;e ,oa izkd`frd xSl ea=ky; esa ea=h \n¼Jh /kesZanz iz/kku½ \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b): ONGC has invited offers to bid out  sixty four operational fields under nomination \nregime and interested agencies have been requested to quote incremental oil/gas, which \ncould be produced from these fields based on their assessment, above the base line \nproduction profile under Business -As-Usual (BAU) scenario . Out of these, sixteen fields \nare from Rajahmundry Asset in Andhra Pradesh . The names of sixteen fields  are \nannexed. \n(c): The technical bids were opened on 17 th January, 2020. The successful bidders have \nmobilization period of 210 days from date of award of contract to start their operation. \n(d): The increased production leads to increased revenues to the State and benefits to the \nlocal economy. \n **** \n \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure to be referred in reply of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 58 to be answered \non 03.02.2020 \n1. Adivipalem \n2. Elamanchili \n3. Razole \n4. Kammapalem \n5. Geddanapalli +TVAA \n6. Mori \n7. Ponamanda + Penumadam \n8. Endamuru \n9. Kaikalur-Vadali \n10. Lingala \n11. Mandapeta  West \n12. Nandigama \n13. Penugonda + North Penugonda \n14. Rangapuram \n15. Ravulapalem \n16. Sitaramapuram \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_59": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.59 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nINFLOW OF TOURISTS \n \n59.  SHRI ANTO ANTONY:  \nSHRI GURJEET SINGH AUJLA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any statistics regarding number \nof foreign and domestic tourists visited various destinations in \nthe country;  \n(b)  if so, the details of foreign and domestic tourists visited \nvarious destinations in the country during the last five years, \nState-wise along  with the revenue generated during the last \nfive years, year-wise;  \n(c)  whether the Government is aware that the annual footfall of \nforeign tourist to India has fallen drastically during the last \nfinancial year causing serious damage to the tourism indust ry \nand if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor along \nwith the steps taken by the Government to boost the tourism \nindustry;  \n(d)  whether the agitations and violence held due to Citizen \nAmendment Act (CAA) has impacted the tourism industry \nalong with the details of the countries which have imposed \nban or issued advisory for its citizens visiting India; and  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b): Details of  Domestic Tourist Visits  (DTV) and Foreign \nTourist Visits (FTV) in the States/UTs for the years 2014 to 2018 are \ngiven in Annexure -I. Data on tourist visits to destinations and \nrevenue generated from tourism are not centrally maintained in the \n\nMinistry of Tourism. However, Foreign Exchange Earned from \ntourism in terms of rupees crore are estimated to be 120367, \n134844, 154146, 177874, 194881 and 210981 for the years 2014, \n2015, 2016, 2017, 2018 and 2019 respectively. \n \n(c): In alignment with the   practice b eing followed by United \nNations World Tourism Organization (UNWTO), Ministry of Tourism, \nGovernment of India maintains data on Foreign Tourist Arrivals \n(FTA) calendar year -wise. The details of FTA for the last two \ncalendar years are given below: \n \nYear 2018 2019 (Provisional) \nFTA(in Millions) 10.56 10.89 \n \n(d) & (e): No assessment has been done regarding impact on \ntourism of agitations held due to Citizen Amendment Act (CAA). \n \nThe details of some countries imposing ban/ issued advisory to \ntheir citizen visiting India is attached at Annexure-II.  \n \n******* \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a ) & (b) OF THE L OK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 59 ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 REGARDING INFLOW OF \nTOURISTS. \n \nDetails of Domestic Tourist  Visits (DTV) and Foreign Tourist  Visits (FTV) \nduring years 2014 to 2018 \n \nStates/UTs 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 (Revised) \nDTV FTV DTV FTV DTV FTV DTV FTV DTV FTV \nAndaman \n& Nicobar \nIslands \n285146 17235 296684 14674 384552 15466 471919 15313 498279 15242 \nAndhra \nPradesh \n93306974 66333 121591054 237854 153163354 341764 165433898 271362 194767874 281083 \nArunachal \nPradesh \n335974 5204 352067 5705 385875 6598 444005 7147 512436 7653 \nAssam 4826702 21537 5491845 24720 5160599 19456 6052667 21760 5856282 36846 \nBihar 22544377 829508 28029118 923737 28516127 1010531 32414063 1082705 33621613 1087971 \nChandigarh 1061419 28365 1073842 29538 1182504 31549 1425781 31832 1538796 39681 \nChhattisgarh 24488465 7777 18327841 6394 16534471 9220 17350030 6701 19329501 14399 \nDadra & \nNagar \nHaveli \n579638 1799 527782 1797 589074 1891 614182 1908 609435 1608 \nDaman & \nDiu \n795167 4620 790911 5858 826201 5669 858131 5535 898824 5694 \nDelhi *  22626859 2319046 25258051 2379169 28460832 2520083 29114423 2740502 29114423 2740502 \nGoa 3544634 513592 4756422 541480 5650061 680683 6895234 842220 7081559 933841 \nGujarat 30912043 235524 36288463 284973 42252909 343752 48343121 448853 54369873 513113 \nHaryana 7467064 314757 7395496 303118 7382995 331291 6050325 193381 488